// @run
// @log 1
// @log 2
// A DEGENERATE union alias — every arm the SAME spelling — is that arm (D446).
//
// `type AB = {a:i32,b:i32} | {a:i32,b:i32}` denotes exactly one struct, but nothing on the
// declaration route dedups: `parseTypeDecl` pushes one variant string per `|` arm and the
// checker's `fillTypeDeclAt` pushes every resolved member, so `udVariants.length` read 2.
// `unionStructAliasShape` gated on `length == 1`, `collectS` never interned a struct row for
// `AB`, `collectU` claimed it as a variant-box union — and `AB[]` became a kind-2 union-BOX
// element list (`refArrElemKind`'s `unNames` arm) while the SAME shape spelled inline stayed
// kind 1. The two reps met at the field store below: `vl check` rc 0 and, from the engine,
// "type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)".
//
// The `| {a:i32,b:i32}` is the whole defect: delete it and this has always run. So the arity
// question is asked of the DISTINCT arms, which puts the declaration back on the struct-alias
// path #2004 built for `type AB = {a:i32} & {b:i32}` — R2 records the SHAPE's arena type at
// the mint, and the row `AB` now has can be twinned like any other.
type AB = {a:i32, b:i32} | {a:i32, b:i32}
type W = { xs: {a:i32, b:i32}[] }
const q: AB[] = [{a:1,b:2}]
const w: W = { xs: q }
print(w.xs[0].a)
print(w.xs[0].b)
